From a564a60924a7795cedc9cc66bbed10a22637e2e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Corey <coreysho@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 14:27:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] XP rate modes: realism, 5x and 10x, chosen once on Tutorial
 Island

THE MULTIPLIER IS IN THE ENGINE, and that is not a shortcut. There are 288
stat_advance call sites across 122 content files and no wrapper proc they pass
through, so anything content-side would have to be added to every one of them
and would be missed by the next skill anybody writes. Player.addXp already had
a world-wide Environment.NODE_XPRATE in exactly the right spot; this makes it
per-player as well:

    const multi = allowMulti ? Environment.NODE_XPRATE * this.xpRate() : 1;

THE VARP HOLDS THE RATE ITSELF (1, 5 or 10), not a mode index, so the engine
needs no table of modes and the three values in gamemode.constant are the only
place they are written down. Player.xpRate() finds the varp by NAME through
VarPlayerType.getId, behind a -2 sentinel because the configs are not loaded
when the class is.

0 means never chosen and reads as 1 - the authentic rate, which is exactly
what the server did before this existed. So every character that already
exists is untouched, a missing varp cannot mean "nobody gains experience", and
a player who closes the chooser without picking is not penalised. There is an
upper rail of 100 because a perm varp written wrong would otherwise be
unbounded.

::setlevel still passes allowMulti=false, so it sets the level asked for
rather than five times it.

WHERE IT IS ASKED. The RuneScape Guide, before the switch on %tutorial rather
than inside one branch, so the welcome, the come-back-later label AND the
skip-the-tutorial branch are all covered by one call. He awards no experience
himself and every step of his comes before the first skilling step, by the
tutorial's own progress numbers.

THE LOCK is the only thing keeping this a choice rather than a button that
says "give me ten times the experience". ~xprate_choose returns early when a
rate is already set AND when %tutorial has reached ^tutorial_complete - the
island is walk-back-to-able, so without the second one a maxed account could
talk to the guide and pick 10x. Both come before the window opens; a lock
after if_openmain would look right and do nothing.

THE WINDOW went through two wrong drafts, both recorded in the generator:

  - each box was a buttontype=select rect so the chosen mode would light
    itself up client-side, with a separate Choose text button inside it. A
    select rect covers that text and takes the click, so it would have
    swallowed the one p_pausebutton was waiting for and suspended the script
    forever. One rect per mode, buttontype=normal, hover via overcolour.
  - the highlight had nothing to highlight: ~xprate_choose returns early once
    a rate is set, so the window is only ever open while it is UNSET. The
    varp's transmit=yes went with it.

It keeps a close button on purpose. Closing aborts the asking script, which
leaves the rate at 0, read as 1x, and the guide asks again - self-healing,
where a mandatory window with no way out can strand a player.

The debugproc for testing the faster modes QUEUES its write, because
ClientCheatHandler runs a debugproc with executeScript(script, FALSE) - a
debugproc has no protected access, the same rule that crashed the Barrows
chest out of its [if_close]. Writing the varp inline is a build error, which
is the build catching it rather than a player finding it.

Drop rates are deliberately not here. 214 hand-written tables with no shared
roll proc, and "a drop-rate boost" has three defensible readings costing very
different amounts; that is its own round once the modes have been played.

Verification: clean build; rs2check 1704 scripts / 495 configs, 0 ERROR
0 CHECK; tools/gamemode_battery.py 64 checks, which reads the ENGINE source
too because half of what matters lives in the other repo;
tools/gamemode_mutate.py 39 mutations, every one caught by its own check;
every other battery still green.

Three of those checks were wrong before they were right: one flagged every
multiply() inside a stat_advance (arrows.rs2 multiplies a count by an xp
value), one asserted the survival guide awards the tutorial's first xp when it
does not call stat_advance at all, and one embedded %xp_rate in a
%-formatted message, where Python read it as a format spec.

